blic from seeing what's  happening and it's particularly  important in situations where  the state is using its power and the  most pure and  serious way possible in killing one of  our citizens  so it's understandable  why the government would not want to be  transparent and why they would pay lip  service to that  but in fact not want to be transparent  but the reality is the constitution  requires them to be transparent  we'll stay on top of this case and bring  you any updates i've also  put in a request to cover the execution  running on immigration everybody knows  that  if you have an open border then you  really aren't protecting the citizens of  this country  up next we talk with republican  congressional district 4 candidate and  trump supporter carolina serrano  plus rebuilding the country is it  possible congress finally has an  infrastructure deal  we'll tell you what's in the work  welcome back to politics now  incumbent congressman stephen horsford  has drawn a few republicans planning to  run against him  one is carolina serrano who's a  colombian-american born in the u.s  she worked on the trump campaign in  nevada and she's a unlv graduate  turns out she was also once an intern  here at channel 8.  john langler talked with her about her  campaign  you've been supported notably you've  gotten some donations already from steve  wynn  how did that happen and obviously given  mr winn's recent  history did you have any hesitation  about taking his donations  i didn't because i worked for one in  encore for almost eight years  and i would say in my 13 years in the  service industry those eight years were  the best ones  and so having his support means a lot i  have a lot of respect for  what his vision and how he's changed las  vegas so i'm happy to have it  you just discussed about your connection  with folks who work in the service  industry in las vegas  we've been through such a difficult time  with the pandemic and the resorts were  shut down for such a long time what do  they need in your opinion what do they  need to get back on their feet to find  employment or if they're already  employed  how to get back on even terms i think  that they  need other options besides the service  industry like i said i mean  it's great for a bit of time but it  doesn't have that longevity that you  need in order for you to be able to  build a home have a family have you know  create wealth i would say so i think  that there's just other  options outside the earth service  industry would be good for them so so  from a policy standpoint then what does  that  what does that look like in terms of  getting them out of the cycle of the  service industry  so i would say you know hospitality is  great if if you want to stay in it but i  would say  i would love to see policies that bring  back manufacturing to  the us right because we don't really  produce much here in the us  and i think you know one of the  industries that i would love to see come  down here would be the fashion industry  i think it would be  such a great place to have that industry  here in vegas where  vegas is kind of this fancy glitzy place  you know your announcement you spoke a  lot about immigration  and build finishing the wall why is that  such an important issue  why do you think the wall needs to be in  your words finished well i think it's  always been a an important issue i mean  i voted democrat and  before they were always about protecting  the border and protecting the country i  think  everybody knows that if you have an open  border then you really aren't protecting  the citizens of this country right it  just becomes  chaotic so the border wall is important  and then there also needs to be a total  immigration overhaul because also the  people who are coming here to  have a better life for themselves  they're not benefiting from an open  border either because each time  a new crop comes in that's lower wages  for  for big business and not beneficial to  the people who are trying to make it  when you say open border the border  isn't necessarily open obviously there  are  there it's not is open a fair way to  describe our borders right now  yeah because i mean if you go down to  like texas i love road tripping so i've  been down to texas arizona  it's vast lands and you see people  crossing through  where there isn't a border there's no  barrier right  and so yeah i mean dhs we know is  overwhelmed  and so i would say that they're  releasing a lot of people into the  country  and they have no date to show up most  people don't show up to the dates  so it's a problem you help support  former president trump in his  re-election campaign  uh how much of that experience would you  bring into  a primary and a general election  campaign how much do you lean  on the former president do you still  support him do you think he should still  be president  well obviously i worked on his campaign  it was a great experience so yes i  support the president  um i think that that experience was one  of the best experiences and i would say  that everyone should  knock doors in their community because  you find out that you have a lot more in  common  with your neighbor than you think and so  i think there's a lot more in common  between  people who are trump supporters and  people who are bernie supporters and you  could  bring them together on certain issues  we have that full interview up at  8newsnow.com so far she's raised about  50  000 for her campaign her other  republican opponent so far  is sam peters putting out the flames  we need more body as bodies and we need  more air support  up next the changes steve sisilak and  other western governors want to see made  to how we tackle wildfires in the west  plus do we finally have a deal our  washington d.c bureau breaks down what's  in a deal on our country's important  infrastructure the governors of nevada  and california  are asking the federal government for  more help and  money to fight wildfires they made the  comments while touring the damage from  the tamarack fire in gardnerville nevada  that fire started on federal land in  california  then spread to nevada it has forced 16  000 people out of their homes both  governors say they are having a  conversation with president biden  later this week this may sound like deja  vu but  congress is one step closer to closing a  deal on the president's massive  1.2 trillion dollar infrastructure plan  a procedural vote passed with 67 yeses  in the normally split senate as it  stands the bill contains  550 billion dollars in new spending  for public works projects members of  both parties say they believe a final  deal  will happen at a time when washington  seems broken  this group of members behind me came  together each of us are deeply committed  to demonstrating to the country and to  the world  that our government can work and it can  work for the people of the united states  of america  john langler talked with our washington  d.c bureau correspondent alexandra  lamone about the next steps for the bill  and what to expect before this it had  only been a small group of bipartisan  senators that had been working on the  deal behind the scenes so  this makes the process more official and  more likely to head on the road to  actually being passed but it doesn't  guarantee that  the full senate still has to come to an  agreement on this one trillion dollar  proposal  and then the measure would need to get  60 votes in order to pass in the u.s  senate  well alex the president biden top  democrats they had much more ambitious  plans 3.5 trillion dollar package they  talked about with  human infrastructure where do things  stand on that  aspect of the discussion right that was  a really  sweeping proposal that the president and  top democrats want to pass that includes  investments in things like  clean and green energy across the  country  and also in those human infrastructure  measures that you mentioned like  free pre-k education and community  college  for all students the democrats were  hoping to pass that  separately with a process called budget  reconciliation  that would only require 50 votes in  order for that to pass  in the senate rather than 60 well it  looks like that is in jeopardy now too  because democratic senator kirsten  cinema of arizona said  she does not support the idea of  spending 3.5  trillion dollars i mean that is just a  massive number so she says she is not on  board  and democrats need every single democrat  on board so  they are now forced to work with her to  either try to scale back or rework the  package into something that she might  support  if they want any hope of passing that at  all  we'll see where things go on capitol  hill alex limon thank you for joining us  you appreciate it  you're watching politics now  so earlier this week i did a story about  a man who's now living in las vegas but  working in southern california  it's about a four-hour drive he's been  doing for more than a year  the dmv told us that in the first six  months of 2021 more than  22 000 californians turned in their  licenses and ids in nevada  you can check out that story at our  website eightnewsnow.com it's gotten a  lot of comments from people  not too happy about the californians  moving to nevada tell us what you think  thanks so much for joining us at  politics now
 

